  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ We split the interval at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ On each open interval, $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-1, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+2, & x>\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)={\\begin{aligned}3, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3, & x>\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\sqrt{x})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ For $x\\in (0,1),$\n\n$$\n{g}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\sqrt{x})}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open piece $(0,1),$ the limit of the derivative as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ For $x\\in (0,1),$\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{1/4},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/4}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open piece $(0,1),$ the limit of the derivative as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\"",
